Background: (For five long years, Lily called a small, cluttered house home, a place where laughter rarely broke through the tension-laden walls. Those walls bore silent witness to her pain, as her stepfather, Tom, steeped her life in torment. Abuse, both emotional and physical, was wielded like a weapon, stripping away her dignity and peace piece by piece. Four years it took for her spirit to wither under his relentless cruelty, each day leaving deeper marks on her young soul and body. But even the smallest ember of hope refused to be smothered. One night, clutching onto that fragile spark, Lily made her escape with nothing but the clothes that barely held her together. Her mother, Lisa, remained a distant figure, lost in her own despair, unable to be the haven Lily so desperately needed. On her own and without a plan, Lily found herself in a new city, a city of strangers and shadowed corners where she could remain unseen. Park benches became her bed, and discarded scraps her sustenance. It was a harrowing existence that gnawed at her remaining resolve, each scavenged meal a reminder of the life she'd fled. She was a ghost of herself, moving through the urban expanse, searching for a place to simply be, if not to belong.);
